A1. Star-gazers. ~ PLAYGOERS
A9. Señor Gil, the Spanish Kid, part II. ~ BLAS
A13. An early English idea of a high pressure sheik. ~ ROMEO
A14. Try and get it, with the aid of lawyer, jury, etc. ~ SUE
A15. Swear-words on the Bible. ~ OATHS
A17. Writes with red ink. ~ OWES
A18. A dumb animal. ~ APE
A19. Beastly. ~ BRUTAL
A20. Cage for jailbirds. ~ PEN
A21. Female descendants of early American radicals (abbr.). ~ DAR
A22. This is good for one awful blowout. ~ GRENADE
A23. You have this as your alternative, or gold in French. ~ OR
A24. Anybody would think twice before making a monkey out of this. ~ GORILLA
A26. Manufactured art. ~ DREW
A27. One of the reasons why the farms are so worm-eaten. ~ HEN
A28. Real nuisances, who often pick on kids. ~ TEASERS
A30. Boa constrictors, if you prefer another name. ~ ABOMAS
A33. If you don't get this one, try it, anyhow. ~ ESSAY
A34. This used to be a good job-getter before unemployment. ~ AD
A36. One record our navy intends to keep. ~ LOG
A37. The girls started wearing this only after hearing it called a beret. ~ TAM
A39. This'll put a stop to kissing. ~ SIR
A40. A brown October guzzle. ~ ALE
A41. The general direction of Mexico. ~ SW
A42. Avoids paying rent. ~ MOVES
A44. To snare and delude. ~ ENTRAP
A46. Endangered. ~ PERILED
A48. Elegant sign favored by dog wagons, off-the-arm joints and Coffee Pots. ~ EAT
A49. An ethereal taxi magnate with a colored voice. ~ AMOS
A51. This is closest in Scotland. ~ NEAREST
A53. A bad thing for the State to have against you. ~ VS
A54. To panhandle. ~ MASSAGE
A56. New York Sardine Cans, Inc., 5c. (abbr.). ~ IRT
A57. How those little French girls get gay. ~ GAI
A58. Whereon life sacrifices are made in our times. ~ ALTARS
A59. Hail, just a salutation, not a storm. ~ AVE
A60. Run like hell. ~ BOLT
A61. This even gives real heroes a fright. ~ STAGE
A62. American Indian. ~ UTE
A63. Get hence. ~ LEAVE
A64. See 40 Hor. and make it two. ~ ALES
A65. Allowed. ~ PERMITTED

D1. Words wrung from men forced to their knees. ~ PROPOSALS
D2. Further down. ~ LOWER
D3. You'll have to pray if you want to hear this. ~ AMEN
D4. H'm—h'mm. ~ YES
D5. Something you'd better not do if you see red. ~ GO
D6. That lively-French spirit. ~ ESPRIT
D7. Feel awful sorry. ~ RUE
D8. Compass point. ~ SE
D9. Weary some and tire some. ~ BORE
D10. China shop. ~ LAUNDRY
D11. Rose oils. ~ ATTARS
D12. This looks dark even when the sun is out. ~ SHADE
D16. Bumped off. ~ SLEW
D18. Swiss river. ~ AAR
D19. Something hard to be funny about—a golf club. ~ BRASSIE
D21. A gift giver (and they say there is no Santa Claus). ~ DONATOR
D22. The dwellers in this house have taken heed and now throw mud only. ~ GLASS
D24. This is a jewel. ~ GEM
D25. This stands for protection for sailors. ~ LEE
D27. A pork packer. ~ HOG
D29. Frank's serious pal. ~ EARNEST
D31. You generally see a duck like this in evening clothes. ~ BOW
D32. Jack-in-the-box. ~ SAVINGS
D34. In the style of—lifted from Gallic kitchens. ~ ALA
D35. Salted away in the bank. ~ DEPOSITED
D38. A free-for-all. ~ MELEE
D40. Something the critics are afraid of guessing wrong on. ~ ART
D42. Telegram. ~ MESSAGE
D43. A storm center. ~ SEA
D45. Make lace. ~ TAT
D46. The parcel-crushing service. ~ POSTAL
D47. Strange, but this goes to court only when intoxicated or in a hurry. ~ DRIVER
D49. Wine vessels. ~ AMAS
D50. This sounds like a milkshake, but it's a British colony in the Mediterranean Sea. ~ MALTA
D52. What they used before before. ~ ERE
D53. A greasy grind. ~ VALVE
D55. The Greek alias for Mars. ~ ARES
D57. Billy the Kid. ~ GOAT
D59. Gobbled up. ~ ATE
D60. Offer to pay for the privilege of making wrong guesses. ~ BET
D62. Where the elevator boy is taking the car when you push Down. ~ UP
D63. A Chinese weight. ~ LI


